# Paths Prettier must not touch. Editors run Prettier on whatever file is open, so this file — and not
# only `scripts/format.sh` — is what keeps the excluded bytes excluded.
#
# The rule for what belongs here: a file whose bytes somebody else owns. `scripts/format.sh` feeds Prettier
# tracked markdown, so these paths would otherwise be formatted by the gate as readily as any other.

# The development workflow's own bytes. `dev-profiles install` materialises the profiles under
# `.dev-profiles/` into `.agents/skills/`, and `dev-profiles doctor` byte-compares what it installed
# against what the library declares — including the executable bits. Reformatted copies read as a broken
# install, and the fix a doctor suggests is to overwrite the formatting.
/.dev-profiles/
/.agents/

# The profile pointer stanza in this file is written and rewritten by `dev-profiles install`, which puts
# no blank line before its closing marker. Prettier puts one there. The two would rewrite each other on
# every run, and the check would fail whichever tool ran first. The rest of the file is 15 lines of prose
# that a formatter earns nothing from.
/AGENTS.md

# Generated. `scripts/gen-third-party-notices.sh` writes it, and `mise run release:check` regenerates it
# and byte-compares, so a formatted copy is a red release for a reason the dependency graph has nothing to
# do with. Regenerate rather than format.
/packaging/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md

# Build and release output, and anything a package manager leaves behind.
/bin/
/dist/
/vendor/
/node_modules/
